To execute these bulk operations using the Salesforce REST API's sObject Collections resource, we can use a `POST` request for the creations with `allOrNone: true`, and a `PATCH` request for the updates with `allOrNone: false`. 

```http
POST /services/data/v58.0/composite/sobjects HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "allOrNone": true,
  "records": [
    {
      "attributes": { "type": "Contact" },
      "AccountId": "0015g00000TrAd3AAB",
      "FirstName": "Maya",
      "LastName": "Chen",
      "Email": "maya.chen@example.com"
    },
    {
      "attributes": { "type": "Contact" },
      "AccountId": "0015g00000TrAd3AAB",
      "FirstName": "Luis",
      "LastName": "Ortega",
      "Email": "luis.ortega@example.com"
    },
    {
      "attributes": { "type": "Contact" },
      "AccountId": "0015g00000TrAd3AAB",
      "FirstName": "Priya",
      "LastName": "Nair",
      "Email": "priya.nair@example.com"
    }
  ]
}

###

PATCH /services/data/v58.0/composite/sobjects HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "allOrNone": false,
  "records": [
    {
      "attributes": { "type": "Account" },
      "id": "0015g00000TrAd3AAB",
      "Rating": "Hot"
    },
    {
      "attributes": { "type": "Opportunity" },
      "id": "0065g00000TrAd9AAB",
      "NextStep": "Send pricing"
    }
  ]
}
```

To meet all the requirements, the best approach is to use the **Composite Graph API**. 

A Composite Graph request allows you to send multiple independent transactions (called graphs) in a single API call. Each graph (representing one order) is fully transactional—meaning if the Opportunity fails to insert, the Account upsert in that same graph will roll back. However, a failure in one graph does not impact the execution of the other graphs in the request. 

```http
POST /services/data/v60.0/composite/graph HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "graphs": [
    {
      "graphId": "SO-1001",
      "compositeRequest": [
        {
          "referenceId": "acct_1001",
          "method": "PATCH",
          "url": "/services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Account/Customer_Number__c/C-1001",
          "body": {
            "Name": "Alder Creek Cafe"
          }
        },
        {
          "referenceId": "opp_1001",
          "method": "POST",
          "url": "/services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Opportunity",
          "body": {
            "Name": "Web order SO-1001",
            "StageName": "Closed Won",
            "CloseDate": "2026-09-26",
            "Amount": 1250,
            "AccountId": "@{acct_1001.id}"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "graphId": "SO-1002",
      "compositeRequest": [
        {
          "referenceId": "acct_1002",
          "method": "PATCH",
          "url": "/services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Account/Customer_Number__c/C-2002",
          "body": {
            "Name": "Birch Lane Bakery"
          }
        },
        {
          "referenceId": "opp_1002",
          "method": "POST",
          "url": "/services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Opportunity",
          "body": {
            "Name": "Web order SO-1002",
            "StageName": "Closed Won",
            "CloseDate": "2026-09-26",
            "Amount": 980,
            "AccountId": "@{acct_1002.id}"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

To create the records in a single call with all-or-nothing semantics where the subrequests are processed sequentially, you should use the standard Composite API. Setting `"allOrNone": true` ensures the transaction rolls back if any subrequest fails, and executing them sequentially ensures the newly created Asset is available to the SOQL query in the `Service_Request__c` trigger.

```http
POST /services/data/v58.0/composite HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "allOrNone": true,
  "compositeRequest": [
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/services/data/v58.0/sobjects/Account",
      "referenceId": "newAccount",
      "body": {
        "Name": "Hollis Outdoor Supply"
      }
    },
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/services/data/v58.0/sobjects/Asset",
      "referenceId": "newAsset",
      "body": {
        "Name": "Trailhead Kiosk 3000",
        "SerialNumber": "SN-88213",
        "AccountId": "@{newAccount.id}"
      }
    },
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/services/data/v58.0/sobjects/Service_Request__c",
      "referenceId": "newRequest",
      "body": {
        "Subject__c": "Installation",
        "Serial_Number__c": "SN-88213"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

To perform an upsert (create or update) using an external ID, use a `PATCH` request to the SObject external ID endpoint. To set a lookup field (like the parent account) via an external ID, use the relationship name (`Parent`) and provide a nested JSON object containing the external ID field and value.

```http
PATCH /services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Account/ERP_Customer_Id__c/ERP-10042 HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "Name": "Hollis Outdoor Supply",
  "Phone": "+1 503 555 0199",
  "BillingCity": "Portland",
  "Parent": {
    "ERP_Customer_Id__c": "ERP-10000"
  }
}
```

